## What is released

Our own contribution: the curated, tiered, provenance-tracked values, and the
code that produces them.

**Note on paths.** `.gitignore` excludes `data/` wholesale — it runs to several gigabytes and contains publisher
PDFs that must not be redistributed. The curated files therefore ship from two *tracked*
directories instead, assembled by `build_release_data.py`. Naming a file under `data/` here would
promise something the repository does not contain.

| released | what it is |
|---|---|
| `release_data/predictions/PAPER_PREDICTIONS.csv` | the five issued predictions (Table 2) |
| `release_data/predictions/CONDITIONAL_PREDICTIONS.csv` | the ten conditional estimates (Table S1) |
| `release_data/results/*.json` | the fitted constants, nulls, ablations and conformal bands |
| `paper/evidence/PROVEN_all_scored.csv` | per-compound blind-test scores |
| `paper/evidence/blind_d2_s*.csv`, `*.json` | the five model seeds behind every seed-averaged figure |
| `paper/evidence/evidence_chain.csv` | each reported number and the file that produces it |
| `paper/` | the manuscript, the supplementary, and every figure script |
| all `*.py` | the pipeline, the analysis and the gates |

`release_data/README.md` carries a manifest with the size and SHA-256 of every released file.

## Provenance: what is traceable, and to what

The corpus table `data/Target_Materials/HEUSLER_KAPPA_TRAINING_SET.csv` carries the provenance
columns `source`, `source_doi`, `source_url` and `source_location`. Precisely:

| | |
|---|---|
| **all 4,094 experimental (tier-0) rows** | carry a `source_doi` — every measured value traces to the publication it came from |
| 94.2 % of all 10,242 rows | carry a `source_url` |
| 60.0 % of all rows | carry a `source_doi`; the remainder are calculated values from databases (AFLOW, Materials Project, JARVIS, OQMD), which are identified by URL rather than by DOI |
| 48.7 % of rows | additionally carry `source_location`, the table or figure within the source |

The derived files — `PAPER_PREDICTIONS.csv`, `CONDITIONAL_PREDICTIONS.csv`, `paper/evidence/*` and
the result JSONs — are **per-compound summaries, not measurement rows**, so they carry no source
DOI: a prediction does not come from a publication. Where they do report source counts, the column
is a count (`n_doi`, and the `doi` column of `evidence_chain.csv`) rather than an identifier. To
trace a number in those files back to literature, join on the compound against the corpus table
above.

## What is NOT released, and why

We do **not** redistribute bulk copies of the upstream databases this corpus draws
on. Those are other people's resources, published under their own terms, and
mirroring them here would neither be ours to do nor useful:

- **Starrydata2** — supplies about 95 % of the experimental rows. Obtain the
  dated snapshot cited in the paper directly from the project; the harvest code
  here rebuilds our tables from it.
- **AFLOW** (AAPL and AGL), **Materials Project**, **JARVIS**, **OQMD**, **COD**
  and OPTIMADE-federated sources — first-principles values and crystal
  structures. Fetched through their public APIs by the scripts here.
- **Publisher PDFs and full-text XML** under `data/pdfs/` — copyrighted by their
  publishers and accessed under institutional subscription. These are excluded
  by `.gitignore` and must never be redistributed.

The scripts regenerate every table from those public sources, so the work is
fully reproducible without us re-hosting anyone else's database.

## Attribution you inherit

If you use these data, you are also using the upstream sources above. Please cite
them as well; the manuscript's reference list names every publication behind
every measurement (198 experimental and 22 computational) precisely so that this
is possible.
